\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Visual anatomy of the six flagship order-four magic squares across Tests~1--4 (Sections~\ref {sec:vectors}--\ref {sec:stacking}). (a)~D\"urer's associative square \#175 (1514, Group~III), whose antipodal pairs sum to $17$ across $O$, partitioning into $12$ outer hull vertices $V_0$ and $4$ inner core vertices $V_1=\{6,7,10,11\}$. (b)~Square \#70 (Group~VI), coloured by concentric rings $R_{\mathrm  {in}},R_{\mathrm  {mid}},R_{\mathrm  {out}}$, where parallel ring dipoles $\mathbf  d_{\mathrm  {out}}=(15,0)=5\mathbf  d_{\mathrm  {in}}$ achieve the fundamental Pell ratio $\|\mathbf  v\|/\|\mathbf  u\|=18/8=9/4\approx \sqrt  {5}$. (c)~Square \#45 ($\kappa =+9$, inverse-radial root $\alpha ^*=-1$, heavy core $\Delta \rho _1/\rho _0=+85/61$), where swapping the single outer border pair $(8,9)\leftrightarrow (15,2)$ between rows~$2$ and~$3$ yields sibling \#54 with the same $(V_0,V_1)=(10,6)$ vertex partition but a nearly hollow core cavity ($\Delta \rho _1/\rho _0=-57/58$). (d)~Square \#46 (Group~VI), shaded by cell quadrupole contribution $z_{ij}x_jy_i$, attaining the global maximum $|Q_{xy}|=60$ ($I_1=110<I_2=230<I_3=340$) and fastest $180^\circ $ Dzhanibekov flip about $y=-x$. (e)~Square \#673 (Group~VI), whose $8$ outer vertices form a U-cradle $V_0$ enclosing an $8$-vertex inverted-T core $V_1$, achieving the minimum solid excess density $\Delta \rho _1/\rho _0=5/4$. (f)~Square \#763 (Group~VI), whose centred $2\times 1$ knight's-move facets $\{13,14,15,16\}$ and $\{1,2,3,4\}$ lock into an area-$5$ mirror-twin tower, and whose single border-pair swap $(16,1)\leftrightarrow (4,13)$ produces the naturally balanced solid \#756 ($\mathbf  c_0=\mathbf  0$).}}{3}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:hero-squares}{{1}{3}{Visual anatomy of the six flagship order-four magic squares across Tests~1--4 (Sections~\ref {sec:vectors}--\ref {sec:stacking}). (a)~D\"urer's associative square \#175 (1514, Group~III), whose antipodal pairs sum to $17$ across $O$, partitioning into $12$ outer hull vertices $V_0$ and $4$ inner core vertices $V_1=\{6,7,10,11\}$. (b)~Square \#70 (Group~VI), coloured by concentric rings $R_{\mathrm {in}},R_{\mathrm {mid}},R_{\mathrm {out}}$, where parallel ring dipoles $\mathbf d_{\mathrm {out}}=(15,0)=5\mathbf d_{\mathrm {in}}$ achieve the fundamental Pell ratio $\|\mathbf v\|/\|\mathbf u\|=18/8=9/4\approx \sqrt {5}$. (c)~Square \#45 ($\kappa =+9$, inverse-radial root $\alpha ^*=-1$, heavy core $\Delta \rho _1/\rho _0=+85/61$), where swapping the single outer border pair $(8,9)\leftrightarrow (15,2)$ between rows~$2$ and~$3$ yields sibling \#54 with the same $(V_0,V_1)=(10,6)$ vertex partition but a nearly hollow core cavity ($\Delta \rho _1/\rho _0=-57/58$). (d)~Square \#46 (Group~VI), shaded by cell quadrupole contribution $z_{ij}x_jy_i$, attaining the global maximum $|Q_{xy}|=60$ ($I_1=110<I_2=230<I_3=340$) and fastest $180^\circ $ Dzhanibekov flip about $y=-x$. (e)~Square \#673 (Group~VI), whose $8$ outer vertices form a U-cradle $V_0$ enclosing an $8$-vertex inverted-T core $V_1$, achieving the minimum solid excess density $\Delta \rho _1/\rho _0=5/4$. (f)~Square \#763 (Group~VI), whose centred $2\times 1$ knight's-move facets $\{13,14,15,16\}$ and $\{1,2,3,4\}$ lock into an area-$5$ mirror-twin tower, and whose single border-pair swap $(16,1)\leftrightarrow (4,13)$ produces the naturally balanced solid \#756 ($\mathbf c_0=\mathbf 0$)}{figure.4}{}}
